{:itonami.blueprint/id "cloud-itonami-isic-960"
 :itonami.blueprint/name "Personal-Care Salon/Service Administrative Coordination"
 :itonami.blueprint/isic-rev4 "960"
 :itonami.blueprint/domain :services/personal-care-coordination
 :itonami.blueprint/license "AGPL-3.0"
 :itonami.blueprint/social-impact [:client-safety :operational-efficiency :administrative-transparency]
 :itonami.blueprint/governor :personalcareops-governor
 :itonami.blueprint/robotics false
 :itonami.blueprint/required-technologies [:identity :forms :dmn :bpmn :audit-ledger]
 :itonami.blueprint/status :public-oss
 :itonami.blueprint/maturity :implemented
 :itonami.blueprint/implemented-slice
 "The five per-op advisor fns (personalcareops.advisor/advise-*,
 unchanged) |- personalcareops.governor (three HARD, permanent checks:
 client-unverified, effect-not-propose, scope-exclusion), a REAL compiled
 langgraph-clj StateGraph (langgraph.graph, personalcareops.operation/
 build), append-only audit ledger (personalcareops.store/ledger +
 append-ledger!, MemStore). Fixes a prior gap: operation.cljc's own
 docstring claimed 'langgraph-clj StateGraph' but the actual code was a
 hand-rolled (let [after-intake (intake ..) ...] (case action ...))
 threading pipeline -- zero require of langgraph.graph, zero use of
 state-graph/add-node/compile-graph anywhere in the file -- and its
 `decide` step could never actually reach the `commit` step (dead code)
 while personalcareops.phase's auto-commit table was defined and
 unit-tested in isolation but never consulted by any decision logic; the
 old `commit` step also never wrote to the audit ledger (only the
 coordination-log), unlike hold/escalate. Now: a genuinely compiled
 StateGraph (:intake -> :advise -> :govern -> :decide -+-> :commit /
 :request-approval -> :commit / :hold) with interrupt-before
 #{:request-approval} + in-memory checkpointer for real human-in-the-loop
 resume, personalcareops.phase's auto-commits-at-phase? genuinely gates
 the :commit route, and :commit durably writes both the coordination-log
 AND the ledger. The Governor (three HARD, permanent, un-overridable
 checks) and the append-only audit ledger for hold/escalate were already
 genuinely wired in the pre-graph pipeline -- both preserved unchanged.
 Five ops (:schedule-service-appointment /
 :coordinate-service-status-update / :coordinate-supply-request /
 :schedule-staff-shift-proposal / :flag-safety-concern), all :effect
 :propose only. :flag-safety-concern ALWAYS escalates to a human operator
 via the compiled graph's :request-approval node (interrupt-before,
 checkpoint-based resume), proven end-to-end by
 test/personalcareops/operation_test.cljk (commit / hard-hold (unverified
 client) / scope-exclusion hold / phase-hold / escalate-approve /
 escalate-reject through the REAL compiled graph), plus a
 `kbb -M:dev:run` demo runner producing real ledger entries for every
 scenario. Test suite rewritten from an unfalsifiable assert+println
 harness (0 deftest/is, unconditionally printed 'All tests passed!'
 regardless of whether the prior assertions held) to real clojure.test
 deftest/is across advisor/governor/phase/store/operation."}
